  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Groups like cyanides and nitrites possess two nucleophilic centres and are called ambident nucleophiles. Actually cyanide group is a hybrid of two contributing structures and therefore can act as a nucleophile in two different ways <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mo>[</mo><mo>-</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>&#8801;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8596;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>:</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>-</mo></msup><mo>]</mo></math>, i.e., linking through carbon atom resulting in alkyl cyanides and through nitrogen atom leading to isocyanides.</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/4f92421c-6d12-43bd-af50-6913177e8195-image.png\" style=\"width: 372px; height: 120px;\" /></p><p>KCN is predominantly ionic and provides cyanide ions in solution. Although both carbon and nitrogen atoms are in a position to donate electron pairs, the attack takes place mainly through carbon atom and not through nitrogen atom since C&mdash;C bond is more stable than C&mdash;N bond. However, AgCN is mainly covalent in nature and nitrogen is free to donate electron pair forming isocyanide as the main product.</p></blockquote>",
    "image": null
  },
